{"task_id": "Mbpp/7", "solution": "def _split_words(text: str) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Splits a string into words by whitespace.\"\"\"\n    return text.split()\n\ndef _filter_by_length(words: list[str], min_length: int) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Filters a list of words to include only those meeting a minimum length.\"\"\"\n    return [word for word in words if len(word) >= min_length]\n\ndef find_char_long(input_string: str) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all words in a string that are at least 4 characters long.\n    Splits the input string into words and filters them by length.\n    \"\"\"\n    words = _split_words(input_string)\n    filtered_words = _filter_by_length(words, 4)\n    return filtered_words"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/9", "solution": "```python\ndef find_Rotations(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the minimum number of rotations (greater than 0) required to get the same string.\n    For example, \"aaaa\" requires 1 rotation.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        # For an empty string, 1 rotation is the minimum positive rotation count\n        # that results in the same string, satisfying \"greater than 0\".\n        return 1\n\n    # Iterate through possible rotation counts `k`, from 1 up to the string's length `n`.\n    for k in range(1, n + 1):\n        # A valid rotation count `k` must evenly divide the string's length `n`,\n        # implying the string is formed by repeating its prefix of length `k`.\n        if n % k == 0:\n            is_repeated_pattern = True\n            # Check if the entire string `s` is a repetition of its prefix `s[0:k]`.\n            # This is done by comparing each character `s[i]` with the corresponding\n            # character in the prefix `s[i % k]`.\n            for i in range(k, n):\n                if s[i] != s[i % k]:\n                    is_repeated_pattern = False\n                    break\n            \n            if is_repeated_pattern:\n                # If the string is a repetition of `s[0:k]`, then `k` is the\n                # minimum number of rotations (and the smallest period) required.\n                return k\n    \n    # This line should logically not be reached for non-empty strings because\n    # `k=n` will always satisfy the condition (the string itself is a pattern that repeats once).\n    # However, it serves as a robust fallback.\n    return n\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/9", "solution": "def find_Rotations(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the minimum number of rotations (greater than 0) required to get the same string.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n\n    # For empty strings or single-character strings, 1 rotation is considered the minimum.\n    if n == 0 or n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    # Iterate through possible rotation counts, starting from 1.\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # Rotate the string by 'i' positions.\n        rotated_s = s[i:] + s[:i]\n        \n        # If the rotated string matches the original, this is the minimum rotation count.\n        if rotated_s == s:\n            return i\n    \n    # This line should ideally not be reached for non-empty strings, \n    # as rotating by n positions will always yield the original string.\n    return n"}
