{"task_id": "Mbpp/160", "solution": "```python\ndef find_solution(a, b, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds integer solutions (x, y) for the linear Diophantine equation ax + by = n.\n    Returns a tuple (x, y) if a solution exists, otherwise returns None.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _gcd(x, y):\n        # Calculates the greatest common divisor of x and y using the Euclidean algorithm.\n        x_abs, y_abs = abs(x), abs(y)\n        while y_abs:\n            x_abs, y_abs = y_abs, x_abs % y_abs\n        return x_abs\n\n    def _extended_gcd(x, y):\n        # Implements the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find g = gcd(x, y) and integers s, t\n        # such that x*s + y*t = g. g is always positive (or 0 if x=y=0).\n        if y == 0:\n            # Base case: gcd(x, 0) = abs(x).\n            # We need x*s + 0*t = abs(x).\n            # If x is non-zero, s = sign(x) (1 for positive, -1 for negative), t = 0.\n            return (abs(x), 1 if x >= 0 else -1, 0) if x != 0 else (0, 0, 0)\n\n        g, s_prime, t_prime = _extended_gcd(y, x % y)\n        s = t_prime\n        t = s_prime - (x // y) * t_prime\n        return g, s, t\n\n    def _validate_solution(test_a, test_b, test_n, test_x, test_y):\n        # Validates that the solution (test_x, test_y) satisfies the equation.\n        return test_a * test_x + test_b * test_y == test_n\n\n    # --- Main find_solution logic ---\n\n    # Case 1: Both coefficients are zero\n    if a == 0 and b == 0:\n        return (0, 0) if n == 0 else None\n\n    # Case 2: One coefficient is zero\n    if a == 0:  # b must be non-zero\n        if n % b == 0:\n            return (0, n // b)\n        return None\n    if b == 0:  # a must be non-zero\n        if n % a == 0:\n            return (n // a, 0)\n        return None\n\n    # Case 3: Both coefficients are non-zero\n\n    # Calculate GCD of a and b\n    g = _gcd(a, b)\n\n    # Check for solvability (n must be divisible by g)\n    if n % g != 0:\n        return None\n\n    # Use Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find x0, y0 such that ax0 + by0 = g\n    # The first return value (gcd_val) is ignored as we already have g\n    _, x0, y0 = _extended_gcd(a, b)\n\n    # Scale the particular solution (x0, y0) to satisfy ax + by = n\n    # This gives one valid particular solution (x_p, y_p)\n    k = n // g\n    x_p = x0 * k\n    y_p = y0 * k\n\n    # Adjust the solution to find a specific canonical form,\n    # typically the one with the smallest non-negative x.\n    # The general solution for x is x = x_p + t * (b // g)\n    \n    mod_val_x_coefficient = b // g # This is the coefficient of t for x's general solution. It is non-zero.\n\n    # Calculate x_final in the canonical range [0, abs(mod_val_x_coefficient) - 1]\n    # Using modulo operator with abs() ensures we handle negative mod_val_x_coefficient correctly\n    x_final = x_p % abs(mod_val_x_coefficient)\n    if x_final < 0: # Ensures x_final is non-negative\n        x_final += abs(mod_val_x_coefficient)\n    \n    # Determine the 't' value that leads to x_final\n    # From x_final = x_p + t * (b // g), we get t = (x_final - x_p) / (b // g)\n    t = (x_final - x_p) // mod_val_x_coefficient\n    \n    # Calculate the corresponding y_final using the same t\n    y_final = y_p - t * (a // g)\n\n    # Validate the solution (optional, but good for correctness assertion in design)\n    # _validate_solution(a, b, n, x_final, y_final)\n\n    return (x_final, y_final)\n```"}
