{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\n\ndef _validate_input(number):\n    \"\"\"Checks if the input is a finite, non-negative numeric value.\"\"\"\n    # Validates input type, finiteness, and ensures it's non-negative for square root calculations.\n    if not isinstance(number, (int, float)):\n        return False\n    if math.isinf(number) or math.isnan(number):\n        return False\n    if number < 0:\n        return False\n    return True\n\ndef _calculate_next_square(number):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the given number.\"\"\"\n    # Determines the base for the next perfect square and returns its square.\n    base = math.floor(math.sqrt(number)) + 1\n    return int(base * base)\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(number):\n    \"\"\"Finds the next perfect square greater than a given number.\"\"\"\n    if not _validate_input(number):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a finite, non-negative numeric value.\")\n    \n    return _calculate_next_square(number)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    This uses a dynamic programming approach by finding the Longest Common Subsequence\n    between the original string and its reverse.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # If the string is empty, the LPS length is 0.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    s_reversed = s[::-1]\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest common subsequence of s[0...i-1]\n    # and s_reversed[0...j-1].\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if s[i - 1] == s_reversed[j - 1]:\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    return dp[n][n]"}
