{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(num: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the leftmost unset bit in the binary representation of num, where 'leftmost' means the\n    highest-indexed unset bit from the number's most significant bit downwards.\n    For example, set_left_most_unset_bit(10) (binary 1010) returns 14 (binary 1110).\n    \"\"\"\n    if num == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Find the position of the most significant bit (MSB)\n    msb_pos = num.bit_length() - 1\n\n    # Iterate from MSB position down to 0\n    # Search for the first (leftmost) unset bit from MSB towards LSB\n    for i in range(msb_pos, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the i-th bit is unset (0)\n        if not (num & (1 << i)):\n            # If unset, set this bit and return the new number\n            return num | (1 << i)\n\n    # If the loop completes, all bits from MSB_pos down to 0 are already set.\n    # The leftmost unset bit is at position MSB_pos + 1.\n    return num | (1 << (msb_pos + 1))"}
