{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef max_product(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence of that array.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n\n    all_subsequences_data = []\n\n    # Generates all possible subsequences by choosing to include or exclude each element,\n    # maintaining their original relative order. Each subsequence is stored as a tuple\n    # (elements, indices) to track both values and their original positions.\n    def _generate_all_subsequences_recursive(arr_orig, index, current_elements, current_indices, results):\n        # Base case: if all elements from the original array have been considered\n        if index == len(arr_orig):\n            # Only non-empty subsequences are considered for product calculation\n            if current_elements:\n                results.append((current_elements[:], current_indices[:]))\n            return\n\n        # Option 1: Include the current element at 'index'\n        current_elements.append(arr_orig[index])\n        current_indices.append(index)\n        _generate_all_subsequences_recursive(arr_orig, index + 1, current_elements, current_indices, results)\n        current_elements.pop()  # Backtrack: remove the element for the next option\n        current_indices.pop()   # Backtrack: remove its index\n\n        # Option 2: Exclude the current element at 'index'\n        _generate_all_subsequences_recursive(arr_orig, index + 1, current_elements, current_indices, results)\n\n    # Checks if a given list of numbers forms a strictly increasing sequence.\n    def _is_strictly_increasing(subsequence_elements):\n        for k in range(len(subsequence_elements) - 1):\n            if subsequence_elements[k] >= subsequence_elements[k + 1]:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    # Start the recursive generation process\n    _generate_all_subsequences_recursive(nums, 0, [], [], all_subsequences_data)\n\n    max_product_val = 0  # Initialize with 0 as numbers are positive\n\n    # Iterate through all generated subsequences, filter for increasing ones, and find the maximum product.\n    for elements, _ in all_subsequences_data:\n        if _is_strictly_increasing(elements):\n            current_product = math.prod(elements)\n            if current_product > max_product_val:\n                max_product_val = current_product\n\n    return max_product_val\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    # Handle edge cases for empty or single-element arrays as per design context.\n    # An empty array results in a product of 0. A single element's product is itself.\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n    if len(arr) == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    # dp_max[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    # dp_min[i] stores the minimum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i,\n    # which is crucial for handling negative numbers (negative * negative = positive).\n    dp_max = list(arr)\n    dp_min = list(arr)\n\n    # Initialize the overall maximum product with the largest single element,\n    # as any individual element is a valid increasing subsequence.\n    max_overall_product = max(arr)\n\n    # Iterate through the array to compute maximum and minimum products for increasing subsequences.\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:  # Check for the increasing subsequence condition\n                if arr[i] > 0:\n                    # If arr[i] is positive, extend with previous max product to maximize.\n                    dp_max[i] = max(dp_max[i], dp_max[j] * arr[i])\n                    # Extend with previous min product to keep track of minimums for future negative multiplications.\n                    dp_min[i] = min(dp_min[i], dp_min[j] * arr[i])\n                elif arr[i] < 0:\n                    # If arr[i] is negative, extend with previous min product to get a larger positive product.\n                    dp_max[i] = max(dp_max[i], dp_min[j] * arr[i])\n                    # Extend with previous max product to get a larger negative (smaller) product.\n                    dp_min[i] = min(dp_min[i], dp_max[j] * arr[i])\n                # If arr[i] is 0, dp_max[i] and dp_min[i] remain 0 (from initialization `list(arr)`),\n                # as any product ending in 0 becomes 0, which is correctly handled.\n\n        # Update the overall maximum product found across all ending positions.\n        max_overall_product = max(max_overall_product, dp_max[i])\n\n    return max_overall_product"}
