{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(array1: list[int], array2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    Uses a binary search approach to achieve optimal time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    m, n = len(array1), len(array2)\n\n    # Ensure array1 is the shorter array to simplify binary search range\n    if m > n:\n        array1, array2 = array2, array1\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # The binary search will be performed on the partition point in array1 (i).\n    # i represents the number of elements taken from array1.\n    # j represents the number of elements taken from array2.\n    # We need i + j = k.\n    # Constraints for i:\n    # 0 <= i <= m (number of elements from array1 cannot exceed its length)\n    # 0 <= j <= n => 0 <= k - i <= n\n    # From k - i <= n => i >= k - n\n    # From k - i >= 0 => i <= k\n    # So, i must be in range [max(0, k - n), min(m, k)]\n\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(m, k)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        i = low + (high - low) // 2  # Partition point in array1\n        j = k - i                    # Partition point in array2\n\n        # Elements immediately before the partition\n        L1 = array1[i - 1] if i > 0 else float('-inf')\n        L2 = array2[j - 1] if j > 0 else float('-inf')\n\n        # Elements immediately after the partition\n        R1 = array1[i] if i < m else float('inf')\n        R2 = array2[j] if j < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if partitions are correct\n        if L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1:\n            # Found the correct partition\n            return max(L1, L2)\n        elif L1 > R2:\n            # Too many elements taken from array1, reduce i\n            high = i - 1\n        else:  # L2 > R1\n            # Too many elements taken from array2, increase i\n            low = i + 1\n\n    # This part should ideally not be reached if k is valid (1 <= k <= m + n)\n    # The problem constraints imply a valid k that will yield a result.\n    return -1 # Should not happen for valid inputs."}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(array1, array2, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays using a binary search approach.\n    This function assumes valid inputs as per the design context requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Ensure array1 is the shorter array to optimize the binary search range.\n    if len(array1) > len(array2):\n        array1, array2 = array2, array1\n\n    m = len(array1)\n    n = len(array2)\n\n    # Define the search space for `partitionX` (number of elements taken from array1).\n    # `low` is the minimum number of elements array1 must contribute.\n    # `high` is the maximum number of elements array1 can contribute.\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(k, m)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        # Calculate `partitionX` and `partitionY` for the current split.\n        partitionX = (low + high) // 2\n        partitionY = k - partitionX\n\n        # Determine the maximum element in the left partition of array1.\n        # Use negative infinity if no elements are taken from array1.\n        maxLeftX = array1[partitionX - 1] if partitionX > 0 else float('-inf')\n\n        # Determine the minimum element in the right partition of array1.\n        # Use positive infinity if all elements are taken from array1.\n        minRightX = array1[partitionX] if partitionX < m else float('inf')\n\n        # Determine the maximum element in the left partition of array2.\n        maxLeftY = array2[partitionY - 1] if partitionY > 0 else float('-inf')\n\n        # Determine the minimum element in the right partition of array2.\n        minRightY = array2[partitionY] if partitionY < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if the current partition is correct.\n        # A correct partition means max element in left half <= min element in right half for both arrays.\n        if maxLeftX <= minRightY and maxLeftY <= minRightX:\n            # If valid, the kth smallest element is the maximum of the two left partition maximums.\n            return max(maxLeftX, maxLeftY)\n        elif maxLeftX > minRightY:\n            # If elements from array1's left partition are too large, reduce partitionX.\n            high = partitionX - 1\n        else: # maxLeftY > minRightX\n            # If elements from array2's left partition are too large, increase partitionX.\n            low = partitionX + 1\n\n    # This part should theoretically not be reached if k is within the valid range [1, m+n].\n    # The problem statement implies valid inputs, so a solution will always be found.\n    return -1 # Placeholder, should not be reached with valid inputs."}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "def armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given number is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number is equal to the sum of its digits, each raised to the power of the number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    num_str = str(number)\n    num_digits = len(num_str)\n    armstrong_sum = 0\n\n    for digit_char in num_str:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        armstrong_sum += digit ** num_digits\n\n    return armstrong_sum == number"}
